# Common UI Conventions

<rule>
actions:
  - type: information
    message: |
      ## Common UI Conventions
      
      This document provides guidelines for developing components and hooks for the Common UI library.
      
      ## Component Guidelines
      
      ### Ground Rules for Components
      
      - ZCD components should live in `src/ZCD`
      - ZCD components should start with the ZCD prefix, for example, `ZCDButton`
      this will help us to distinguish between local project components and ZCD components
      - ZCD components should not accept `style` prop, and every variant should be controllable through props
      - Every component should have a storybook stories file for easy visual testing. For detailed Storybook guidelines, please refer to the `storybook-conventions.mdc` rule.
      - All component should use `CSS modules` for their styles, CSS module file names should have the `.module.scss` postfix
      - Use CSS Logical Properties like `padding-inline-end` instead of `padding-right` or `inset-inline-start`, instead of `left`, this will help us build better bidirectional
      - Export your component from `src/index.ts` like so, to make them available to users: `export { ZCDDialog } from './ZCDDialog/ZCDDialog';`
      
      ### Using Internal Components First
      
      When building new features or components:
      
      - **Always prioritize using our internal component library first** before creating new components or using third-party alternatives
      - Check the `src/index.ts` file to discover available components that can be imported directly
      - For UI elements such as buttons, inputs, or layout components, use our ZCD components (e.g., `ZCDButton`, `ZCDInput`, etc.)
      - Only create new components when existing ones don't meet your requirements after careful consideration
      - If extending functionality, consider enhancing existing components rather than creating new ones
      - Maintain consistency across the library by leveraging our established component patterns
      
      ```tsx
      // Good: Using internal ZCDButton component
      import { ZCDButton } from '@zencity/common-ui';
      
      function MyFeature() {
        return <ZCDButton text="Submit" variant="primary" />;
      }
      
      // Avoid: Creating a custom button or using third-party alternatives
      function MyFeature() {
        return <button className="custom-button">Submit</button>;
      }
      ```
      
      ### Class Names in Components
      
      When adding class names to your components, it is important to adhere to certain guidelines to maintain consistency and readability in your codebase.
      
      Here are some things to consider:
      
      - **Root Component Class Name:** The **`className`** prop should be used for the root component. This gives consumers of your component the flexibility to add their own styling if required.
      
      ```tsx
      return (
          <div
            className={classNames(
              styles.card,
              className, // <-- Add className prop to root component
            )}
          >
            { /* ...rest of the component */ }
          </div>
        );
      ```
      
      ### Creating Component Storybook Stories
      
      Every component should have a well-structured Storybook stories file. Create stories following the `storybook-conventions.mdc` guidelines which provide comprehensive structure for consistent, high-quality stories.
      
      Your stories should include:
      
      1. Detailed prop documentation
      2. Multiple component variations
      3. Interactive tests with play functions
      4. Accessibility testing
      
      The story file should be placed in the same directory as the component and have the same name with `.stories.tsx` extension.
      
      ## Hook Guidelines
      
      ### Ground Rules for Hooks
      
      - Common UI hooks should live in `src/hooks`
      - Hook names should always start with `use` prefix, following React's naming convention
      - Hooks should be focused on a single responsibility
      - Every hook should be TypeScript-based with full type coverage
      - Export hooks from `src/index.ts` to make them available to consumers
      - Every hook should have thorough documentation and examples
      - Every hook should have comprehensive tests with at least 90% coverage
      - Every hook should have a Storybook story demonstrating its usage
      
      ### Hook Development Process
      
      #### 1. Research & Planning
      
      Before creating a new hook, thoroughly check:
         - Existing hooks directory for similar functionality
         - Related components for hooks that could be extracted and reused
         - Open source packages that might serve the need (but prefer building our own)
      
      #### 2. Implementation
      
      When implementing a new hook:
         - Create a new file in the `src/hooks` directory with the hook name (e.g., `useFormField.ts`)
         - Implement with TypeScript typing for ALL parameters and return values
         - Add JSDoc documentation above the hook function
         - Include inline comments for complex logic
         - Optimize for performance where appropriate
      
      #### 3. TypeScript Structure
      
      Ensure strong TypeScript typing:
      
      ```typescript
      /**
       * Hook description explaining purpose and use cases
       *
       * @param param1 - Description of param1
       * @param param2 - Description of param2
       * @returns Description of return value
       *
       * @example
       * // Basic usage example
       * const result = useMyHook(param1, param2);
       */
      export function useMyHook<T>(param1: string, param2: T): HookReturnType<T> {
        // Implementation
        return {
          // Typed return values
        };
      }
      
      // Return type interface
      export interface HookReturnType<T> {
        value: T;
        setValue: (newValue: T) => void;
        reset: () => void;
      }
      ```
      
      #### 4. Testing Requirements
      
      For each hook:
      - Write unit tests for all functionality using Vite as the test runner
      - Include tests for expected behavior, edge cases and error handling
      - Mock dependencies as needed
      - Aim for 90%+ test coverage
      - Test each possible return value and parameter
      
      #### 5. Hook Storybook Stories
      
      Create a Storybook story demonstrating the hook's usage:
      
      - Place the story in the same directory as the hook
      - Follow the storybook-conventions structure strictly
      - Demonstrate all major use cases and variants
      - Include interactive examples showing reactive behavior
      - Implement comprehensive play functions for interaction testing
      
      #### 6. Hook Documentation
      
      Provide comprehensive documentation:
      
      - JSDoc comments for the hook and its parameters
      - Detailed usage examples
      - Edge cases and limitations
      - Performance considerations
      - Related hooks or alternatives
      
      ### Hook Export Structure
      
      Export hooks appropriately:
      
      ```typescript
      // In the hook file (e.g., src/hooks/useFormField.ts)
      export function useFormField() {
        // implementation
      }
      
      // In src/index.ts
      export { useFormField } from './hooks/useFormField';
      ```
      
      ### Hook Naming Conventions
      
      - Hook names: `use` + descriptive noun or verb + optional qualifier (e.g., `useFormField`, `useLocalStorage`, `useClickOutside`)
      - Parameters: Clear, descriptive names that indicate usage
      - Return properties: Descriptive names following React conventions
      
      ### Hook Architecture Guidelines
      
      #### Custom Hook vs. Component
      
      Use a hook when:
      - The logic is stateful and can be reused across multiple components
      - It manages lifecycle, side effects, or stateful logic
      - No UI rendering is needed
      
      Use a component when:
      - The feature has a UI aspect
      - The feature combines multiple hooks and UI elements
      - The structure is primarily presentational
      
      #### Hook Dependencies
      
      - Minimize external dependencies
      - Prefer React built-in hooks for composition
      - Document all dependencies and version requirements
      - Consider performance implications of hook composition
      
      ### Hook Performance Considerations
      
      - Use React's built-in performance hooks (`useMemo`, `useCallback`) only when necessary
      - Avoid unnecessary re-renders by memoizing expensive computations
      - Be mindful of hook dependencies to prevent infinite loops
      - Consider using refs for values that shouldn't trigger re-renders
      
      ### Hook Error Handling
      
      Implement robust error handling:
      
      - Gracefully handle edge cases
      - Provide clear error messages
      - Consider returning error states as part of the hook's return value
      - Use try/catch blocks for async operations

